# Do you still keep in touch with your friends from childhood? Why or why not?
Yes, I still keep in touch with a few of my childhood friends, although not as often as before. As we've grown older, everyone has become busy with their studies, careers, and personal lives, so it's naturally more difficult to meet regularly.

That being said, we still stay connected through social media and messaging apps. Occasionally, we arrange a get-together whenever we have free time. I really value these friendships because we've known each other for such a long time and shared many memorable experiences growing up.

I think childhood friends are special because they understand where you came from and have witnessed different stages of your life. Being around them always brings back a lot of good memories and gives me a sense of familiarity and comfort.

# How important is childhood friendship to children?
I think childhood friendships are extremely important because they play a major role in a child's social and emotional development. Through interacting with friends, children learn valuable skills such as communication, teamwork, sharing, and conflict resolution.

In addition, having close friends can give children a sense of belonging and emotional support. During difficult times, friends can help them feel less lonely and more confident. Childhood friendships also create many happy memories and can have a positive impact on a child's self-esteem.

Overall, I believe that strong friendships during childhood help children develop important life skills and contribute to their overall well-being.

# What do you think of communicating via social media?
I think social media has become an extremely convenient way for people to communicate, especially in today's fast-paced world. It allows us to stay in touch with friends, family members, and colleagues regardless of where they are, which is particularly useful for people who live far apart.

Another advantage is that communication through social media is instant and accessible. People can exchange messages, make video calls, or share updates within seconds. This makes it much easier to maintain relationships despite busy schedules.

However, I don't think social media should completely replace face-to-face interaction. Online communication often lacks personal connection and can sometimes lead to misunderstandings because people cannot see each other's facial expressions or body language.

Overall, I believe social media is a useful communication tool, but it should be used alongside real-life interactions rather than replacing them entirely.

# Do you think online communication through social media will replace face-to-face communication?
No, I don't think it will completely replace face-to-face communication, although it will continue to play an increasingly important role in our daily lives.

One reason is that in-person communication allows people to express emotions more effectively through facial expressions, body language, and tone of voice. These non-verbal cues help us understand each other better and build stronger personal relationships.

In addition, face-to-face interactions often feel more genuine and meaningful. For example, spending time with friends or family in person usually creates a stronger sense of connection than simply exchanging messages online.

That being said, social media is still a valuable communication tool. It helps people stay connected regardless of distance and makes communication faster and more convenient. In my opinion, online communication is more likely to complement face-to-face interaction rather than replace it entirely.

# What's the difference between having younger friends and older friends?
I think both younger and older friends can bring different benefits to a person's life.

Younger friends are often more energetic, adventurous, and open to trying new things. They may introduce you to the latest trends, technologies, or forms of entertainment, which can help you stay updated and broaden your perspective.

On the other hand, older friends tend to have more life experience and can offer valuable advice when it comes to education, careers, or personal challenges. They often have a more mature outlook on life, which can be helpful when making important decisions.

That being said, I don't think age is the most important factor in a friendship. What really matters is having shared interests, mutual respect, and a genuine connection. I've met people of different ages who have become great friends despite the age gap.

# Has technology changed people's friendships? How?
Yes, I believe technology has changed friendships in many ways, both positively and negatively.

On the positive side, technology has made it much easier for people to stay in touch. Through social media, messaging apps, and video calls, friends can communicate instantly regardless of where they live. This is especially beneficial for people who move to different cities or countries but still want to maintain close relationships.

However, there are also some downsides. Nowadays, some people rely too heavily on online communication and spend less time interacting face-to-face. As a result, friendships can sometimes become more superficial, as online conversations may not provide the same level of emotional connection as in-person interactions.

Overall, I think technology has transformed the way people build and maintain friendships. While it offers greater convenience, it's important to balance online communication with real-life social interactions.
